WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.659
Film Courage: We talked earlier about where
there was a time where you got kind of stuck

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:05.930
in this place where you could not really move.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:08.930
You didn’t want to do this, you did not
want to do that and you did not know it was

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:09.930
depression.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.660
Can you talk about what happened?

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.900
Dui Jarrod, Writer/Director: When I started
to understand what depression was, there is

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:21.029
the clinical definition when you really get
down to it is you’re unable to make a decision

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:23.150
to move either forward or backwards.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.270
You’re really stuck in this very specific
place.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:30.800
When I first moved to New York I didn’t
realize that I had been going through such

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.980
a depression because I wasn’t able to write…a
play, an essay, anything.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:38.829
I wasn’t able to do anything.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:45.010
And I also wasn’t able to make a decision
about what my life was about to look like.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:50.100
When I started to deal with that I definitely
decided to get with a therapist and work through

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.950
the process of where I was and where I was
eventually trying to go.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:01:00.360
And after being able to identify a lot of
decisions that I was making in the present

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.609
was rooted in a lot of stuff that I had done
and deal with in the past, like losing my

00:01:04.609 --> 00:01:09.880
job and trying to prove to people that I had
worthiness even though something very valuable

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:12.609
to me was actually taken away.

00:01:12.609 --> 00:01:19.510
One of the things that the therapist said
that really began to transform me was 1) He

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:23.289
said “Nobody is responsible for your life
but you.

00:01:23.289 --> 00:01:29.329
And how you engage and get back to you is
you do the things that you want to do.”

00:01:29.329 --> 00:01:32.799
The things that you enjoy, the things that
make you happy.

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:37.579
And through that you will be able to kind
of start seeing the light at the end of the

00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:38.579
tunnel.

00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:41.829
That’s when I started to go to as much theater
as I could in New York.

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:49.240
It was like a seed that had been watered where
it was like I was dead in my creative ability

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:54.640
and I started to really flourish and start
coming up with ideas and started to see the

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.980
definition of who I was.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:02.000
There was one specific play called Sunset
Baby written by Dominique Morisseau that I

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.759
think really defined me and my personal journey
because she was trying to just go get on a

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:12.700
flight and get away and that is all I was
ever trying to do was kind of get away and

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.879
escape the past instead of embracing the past
and using it as fuel to create everything

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:19.239
in the present.

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:23.020
Just kind of what I went through and how I
was able to get back on the other side.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.290
Fortunately I have not experienced that in
a long time.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.019
Film Courage: Sunset Baby?

00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:28.019
Dui: Sunset Baby.

00:02:28.019 --> 00:02:29.540
Film Courage: And you saw this in New York?

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.370
Dui: I saw it in New York at the LAByrinth
Theater.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:37.480
It was unexpected but it was such a great
piece of poetic work.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:38.629
It was just tremendous.

00:02:38.629 --> 00:02:41.750
Film Courage: Did you reach out to the playwright?

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.170
Dui: No I never did.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:47.390
I never reached out and it’s interesting
enough because I have met other writers who

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:53.590
know exactly who she is and so maybe the Universe
will align and I will one day see her and

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:58.330
look at her and say “This play really helped
heal the journey that I had internally.”

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:03.739
Film Courage: It helped heal but you also
wanted to write plays like that?

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:09.120
Dui: That were affecting and I understood
that what made it so affective is that it

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.900
came from a very real place inside of her
and again I wasn’t in a writing place because

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.950
I was trying to hide so much of who I was
from the rest of the world I wasn’t able

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.640
to authentically tell these really deep and
rich stories.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:28.109
Once I started to be okay with me and who
I was I was able to go out and start really

00:03:28.109 --> 00:03:32.870
telling stories in an authentic and rich way.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:37.810
While it has not necessarily caught on to
the popular culture, people that have been

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.090
able to witness my work have been affected
by it and that is what I am grateful for.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:45.220
Film Courage: Do you remember what it was
like leaving the theater after you watched

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.220
it?

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:47.220
Were you in one of these dazes?

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.510
Dui: I was!

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:53.279
It was hard to get to that theater too because
that theater is not by the train.

00:03:53.279 --> 00:03:57.459
And I remember not wanting to leave the theater.

00:03:57.459 --> 00:04:01.469
I didn’t really want to leave the theater
so I remember sitting there for just a half

00:04:01.469 --> 00:04:05.900
second longer than what I should have and
then immediately we went across the street

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:10.900
and I wanted to regurgitate and just talk
about the piece and talk about the journey.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:16.650
And it’s been so many amazing plays that
I have seen in New York, so many incredible

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:22.740
plays and playwrights that people would probably
never see their work staged but it’s an

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.639
incredible group of writers and talent to
pull from in New York.

